# CSS Selectors & Fundamentals

Cascading Style Sheets (CSS) controls the visual presentation, color scheme, and typography of your HTML markup. A CSS rule set consists of a selector pointing to HTML elements, followed by a declaration block containing property and value pairs.

## Common CSS Selectors

To apply styles effectively, you must understand how selectors target elements:
- Element Selectors: Target tags directly by name (e.g., `p`, `h1`, `button`). These apply universally to every matching tag.
- Class Selectors: Target elements sharing a `class` attribute, prefixed with a dot (e.g., `.highlight`, `.card`). Classes are reusable across multiple elements.
- ID Selectors: Target a unique element by its `id` attribute, prefixed with a hash (e.g., `#main-header`). An ID must be unique within a single document.

```css
/* Element selector */
p {
  color: #334155;
  font-size: 16px;
}

/* Class selector */
.callout {
  background-color: #f1f5f9;
  border-left: 4px solid #2563eb;
  padding: 12px;
}

/* ID selector */
#main-nav {
  background-color: #0f172a;
}
```

## Understanding Specificity

When multiple CSS rules conflict on the same element, the browser resolves the conflict using specificity:
1. Inline styles written in the HTML tag take highest priority.
2. ID selectors carry high weight.
3. Class, attribute, and pseudo-class selectors carry medium weight.
4. Element and pseudo-element selectors carry the lowest weight.

As a best practice, prefer class selectors for styling. Overusing ID selectors makes your stylesheet rigid and difficult to maintain.

## Key takeaways
- CSS declarations consist of properties and values wrapped inside curly brackets.
- Use element selectors for baseline typography defaults.
- Use class selectors (`.class-name`) for reusable component styles.
- Keep specificity low and balanced by relying primarily on classes rather than IDs.

starterCode.css:
#page-title {
  color: #2563eb;
}

p {
  color: #475569;
  font-size: 16px;
}

.featured {
  color: #16a34a;
  font-weight: bold;
}
